<p class="lead">If you plan to stay in your home for the long haul, a metal roof may be the last roof you ever buy. Austin Roofing installs and repairs Austin metal roofing systems — from crisp standing seam on modern builds to rugged ribbed panels on Hill Country barns and stone-coated steel that looks like tile or shake — and we will tell you honestly whether metal makes sense for your home and budget.</p>

<p>Metal costs more up front than composition shingles, but it sheds heat, stands up to wind, and can outlast two or three shingle roofs. The key is choosing the right profile and installing it with the details metal demands: proper clips, sealed laps, and flashing that allows for expansion in the Texas sun.</p>

<h2>Metal Roofing Systems We Install in Austin</h2>
<h3>Standing seam metal</h3>
<p>Standing seam panels run from eave to ridge with raised, interlocking seams. The fasteners sit underneath the panel, attached with clips, so there are no exposed screws to fail and the panels can expand and contract freely. It is the premium choice for homes: clean lines, excellent weather resistance and the longest service life of any common residential roof. It also suits lower pitches than shingles can handle.</p>
<h3>Exposed-fastener metal (R-panel and similar)</h3>
<p>These ribbed panels are screwed directly through the face into the deck or purlins. They cost less than standing seam and are popular for farmhouse-style homes, workshops, barns and carports. The trade-off is maintenance: the rubber washers on the screws age in UV and heat, and fasteners typically need inspection and replacement at intervals over the life of the roof to prevent leaks.</p>
<h3>Stone-coated steel</h3>
<p>Stone-coated steel panels are formed steel with a coating of bonded stone granules, shaped to resemble clay tile, cedar shake or dimensional shingles. They give you a traditional look with the strength and low weight of steel, and many products carry strong impact and wind ratings — a good option for homeowners and HOAs that do not want a "metal" appearance.</p>

<h2>Metal vs Shingles: How They Compare</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
